        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: When was Bartholomew Green born?\n\nAnswer A: 1699\n  about: Bartholomew Green\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Bartholomew Green (printer, born 1699)\" [document 3]\n\nAnswer B: 1666\n  about: Bartholomew Green\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Bartholomew Green ( printer , born 1666 ) , American printer and publisher\" [document 5]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Bartholomew Green (printer, born 1666) Bartholomew Green (October 12, 1666 – December 28, 1732) was a printer at and later the publisher of The Boston News-Letter. He was born in Cambridge, Massachusetts. The Boston News-Letter is regarded as the first continuously published newspaper in British North America. Initially, it was heavily subsidized by the British government, with a limited circulation. The News-Letter’s first editor was John Campbell. In 1722 the editorship passed to Green, the paper’s printer. Green changed the focus of the newspaper to place more emphasis on domestic events. After his death in 1732 his son John Draper,\nDocument 2: Bartholomew Green (printer, born 1700) Bartholomew Green (January 1, 1700 – December 28, 1732) was a printer at and later the publisher of The Boston News-Letter. He was born in Cambridge, Massachusetts. The Boston News-Letter is regarded as the first continuously published newspaper in British North America. Initially, it was heavily subsidized by the British government, with a limited circulation. The News-Letter’s first editor was John Campbell. In 1722 the editorship passed to Green, the paper’s printer. Green changed the focus of the newspaper to place more emphasis on domestic events. After his death in 1732 his son John Draper,\nDocument 3: Bartholomew Green (printer, born 1699) Bartholomew Green Jr. (1699 or 1701 – October 29, 1751) was a son of Bartholomew Green, printer of the \"Boston News-Letter\". Green married Hannah Hammond in 1724 and they had five children. He apprenticed with his father until he went on his own in 1725. Almost immediately he began printing the \"Boston Gazette\" which was a rival to his father's \"Boston News-Letter\". He continued to print this newspaper until 1732. He also worked with the firm of Bushell, Allen and Green and developed a reputation for high quality printing. A commission in the British army\nDocument 4: Bartholomew Green (martyr) Bartholomew or Bartlet Green (1530–1556), was an English Protestant martyr. Green was born in the parish of Basinghall, city of London. He was of a wealthy Roman Catholic family, and at the age of sixteen was sent by his parents, 'who favoured learning,' to Oxford University, proceeding B.A. in 1547. At the university he was a laborious student, and was converted by Peter Martyr's lectures to the Protestant religion. On leaving Oxford Green entered the Inner Temple, and after a period of dissipation his earlier impressions revived, and he gave up his worldly amusements. His family were\nDocument 5: information Cite this page Get shortened URL Download QR code Print/export Download as PDF Printable version In other projects Wikidata item Appearance move to sidebar hide From Wikipedia , the free encyclopedia Bartholomew Green may refer to : Bartholomew Green ( martyr ) ( 1530–1556 ) , English Protestant martyr Bartholomew Green ( printer , born 1666 ) , American printer and publisher Bartholomew Green ( printer , born 1699 ) ( 1699–1751 ) , son of Bartholomew Green , printer of the Boston News-Letter Bartholomew Green , Essex , a hamlet in Felsted , England Topics referred to by\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
